T2  - Proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning
AB  - Federated learning harnesses the power of distributed optimization to train a unified machine learning model across separate clients. However, heterogeneous data distributions and computational workloads can lead to inconsistent updates and limit model performance. This work tackles these challenges by proposing FedECADO, a new algorithm inspired by a dynamical system representation of the federated learning process. FedECADO addresses non-IID data distribution through an aggregate sensitivity model that reflects the amount of data processed by each client. To tackle heterogeneous computing, we design a multi-rate integration method with adaptive step-size selections that synchronizes active client updates in continuous time. Compared to prominent techniques, including FedProx, FedExp, and FedNova, FedECADO achieves higher classification accuracies in numerous heterogeneous scenarios.
