PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning
AB  - Label distribution learning (LDL) is a novel machine learning paradigm that can handle label ambiguity. This paper focuses on the interpretability issue of label distribution learning. Existing local interpretability models are mainly designed for single-label learning problems and are difficult to directly interpret label distribution learning models. In response to this situation, we propose an improved local interpretable model-agnostic explanations algorithm that can effectively interpret any black-box model in label distribution learning. To address the label dependency problem, we introduce the feature attribution distribution matrix and derive the solution formula for explanations under the label distribution form. Meanwhile, to enhance the transparency and trustworthiness of the explanation algorithm, we provide an analytical solution and derive the boundary conditions for explanation convergence and stability. In addition, we design a feature selection scoring function and a fidelity metric for the explanation task of label distribution learning. A series of numerical experiments and human experiments were conducted to validate the performance of the proposed algorithm in practical applications. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed algorithm achieves high fidelity, consistency, and trustworthiness in explaining LDL models.
