TI  - RATE: Causal Explainability of Reward Models with Imperfect Counterfactuals
AU  - Reber, David
AU  - Richardson, Sean M
AU  - Nief, Todd
AU  - Garbacea, Cristina
AU  - Veitch, Victor
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning
AB  - Reward models are widely used as proxies for human preferences when aligning or evaluating LLMs. However, reward models are black boxes, and it is often unclear what, exactly, they are actually rewarding. In this paper we develop Rewrite-based Attribute Treatment Estimator (RATE) as an effective method for measuring the sensitivity of a reward model to high-level attributes of responses, such as sentiment, helpfulness, or complexity. Importantly, RATE measures the <em>causal</em> effect of an attribute on the reward. RATE uses LLMs to rewrite responses to produce imperfect counterfactuals examples that can be used to measure causal effects. A key challenge is that these rewrites are imperfect in a manner that can induce substantial bias in the estimated sensitivity of the reward model to the attribute. The core idea of RATE is to adjust for this imperfect-rewrite effect by rewriting <em>twice</em>. We establish the validity of the RATE procedure and show empirically that it is an effective estimator.
